# TODO

- Replace the current macOS DNS resolver source with a native implementation
  The current Darwin path relies on a third-party parser over `scutil --dns` output to discover scoped resolvers for ECH and advanced DNS options. This works, but it should be replaced with a native macOS resolver implementation backed by system APIs so the client no longer depends on shelling out or text parsing for resolver discovery.

- Add an explicit WebTTY termination control message
  WebTTY already supports stdin EOF through stream closure, and transport/session close tears down the server-side process. A future protocol revision should add an intentional client-to-server termination message, optionally carrying a signal where the platform supports it, so SDKs can distinguish a deliberate process termination from a network failure without inferring intent from WebSocket close.

- Add targeted WebTTY runtime server resolution
  `webtty exec`, attach, and related runtime commands must resolve registered-server identity, E2E policy, host keys, and capabilities before opening a session. They currently obtain that metadata by listing every online tunnel and matching the requested URL locally. Add a project-scoped Engine endpoint that resolves one tunnel ID, server ID, name, or hostname and returns the same authorized metadata with explicit ambiguous/not-found errors. Keep the inventory API for listing workflows, but remove its use from single-server runtime resolution once the targeted endpoint has equivalent security and compatibility coverage.

- Keep WebTTY E2E crypto agility scoped and explicit
  The protocol reserves ChaCha20-Poly1305 suite identifiers, but the current implementation intentionally supports only the AES-256-GCM payload suite and HPKE X25519 / HKDF-SHA256 / AES-256-GCM key envelopes. Do not enable ChaCha20-Poly1305 until all SDKs have audited implementations, shared test vectors, cross-SDK runtime coverage, and product documentation that explains suite negotiation and fail-closed behavior.

- Keep the WebTTY cybersecurity audit current
  The 2026-06-24 audit is stored outside the repository in the internal release reports. Update it before release when the WebTTY protocol, endpoint authentication, workspace-managed trust, recording encryption, log export, permissions, CLI/config/env surfaces, or cross-SDK runtime behavior change.

- Keep cross-SDK WebTTY mutual-auth coverage synchronized
  The Go WebTTY client/server enforce protocol-bound server authenticity, client authenticity, freshness, and downgrade resistance for E2E sessions. Keep the transcript format synchronized with the JS and C++ SDKs, and retain cross-SDK runtime tests for explicit-key and workspace-managed trust providers before declaring the protocol stable.

- Design a secure WebTTY registered-server identity rotation workflow
  A registered WebTTY server whose local identity is lost currently needs a new server record. That is intentional for now: a simple reset path would make server-id takeover too easy. A future workflow may add explicit owner/admin approval, old-key revocation, audit events, and engine-side admission checks so replacing a server identity stays secure and observable.

- Add ConPTY-backed WebTTY server support on Windows
  The Go WebTTY server can run Windows command sessions in pipe mode, and the Windows runtime suite covers spawn/login command execution, E2E, and C++ interop. It still cannot be the default interactive demo/runtime on Windows because `github.com/creack/pty` returns `ErrUnsupported` on Windows, while `rstream ui` and browser terminal sessions request `allocate_tty`. Keep the Windows demo script on the C++ server by default until the Go server has a real ConPTY backend, resize support, shutdown semantics, and runtime coverage for interactive terminal applications.

- Allow restricting the MCP tool surface when serving/publishing
  `rstream mcp serve` and `rstream mcp publish` expose the full tool set unconditionally (`mcpTools()` in `cmd/rstream/cmd/mcp.go`), including powerful tools such as `rstream_token_create`, `rstream_project_delete`, and `rstream_webtty_exec`. The only differentiation is a computed `readOnly` annotation, which is advisory, not enforced. This is fine for a trusted local agent (Codex on the operator's workstation) but unsafe to hand to a less-trusted consumer such as a browser-driven chat agent that ingests untrusted user input — a prompt injection could mint tokens or delete a project. Today the consumer must allow-list tools client-side, which is bypassable by anyone holding the published MCP token. Add server-side surface restriction via CLI args (for example `--tools webtty,project_list` to expose an explicit set, `--read-only` to expose only the read-only-annotated tools, and/or named profiles), so the exposed subset is enforced at the source. This is what makes a published rstream MCP safe to delegate to a browser agent (see the private-llm-mesh sample) versus a trusted workstation.
